  +val: env_out_acc, in_splits, train_envs, val_X, val_acc, val_data, val_gen, val_reader
  +ood: out_splits, target_data, test_X, test_data, test_env, test_envs, test_gen, test_in_acc, test_out_acc, test_reader
  +eval_fns: accuracy, predict, predict_proba, print_metrics_binary, run_acc, score, sweep_acc, test, validate
  +train_fns: Trainer, fit, main, run_experiment, train, train_epoch, train_step
  +hints: acc, collect, hparam, hyperparam, main, run, select

DomainBed            model_selection.py           expected=FLAG  verdict=INDETERMINATE  -> FN (no sweep recognised)
DomainBed            sweep.py                     expected=clean verdict=INDETERMINATE  -> no credit (no sweep recognised)
mimic3-benchmarks    main.py                      expected=clean verdict=INDETERMINATE  -> no credit (no sweep recognised)
mimic3-benchmarks    main.py                      expected=clean verdict=INDETERMINATE  -> no credit (no sweep recognised)

POST-EXTENSION (fitted):  TP=0  FP=0  FN=1  TN=0   (recognised a sweep in 0/4 files)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The extended vocabulary recognised a sweep in NO additional file. Detector 3's failure on external code is therefore STRUCTURAL, not lexical: it looks for a function whose name carries a sweep hint, which calls a training function inside a loop, and which then calls an evaluation function whose loader argument can be traced to a named val/OOD variable. Third-party selection code does not take that shape -- DomainBed selects by argmax over a table of recorded metrics, with no training call anywhere in the selecting function. Renaming would not have fixed this, so the post-extension arm is reported as a NEGATIVE RESULT rather than as a fitted improvement.
